[Music]  the soft reboot that was Assassin's  Creed origins introduced a new approach  to the series's brand of stealth action  gameplay along with an expansive and  vibrant open world with many dynamic  systems at work in this year's follow up  Assassin's Creed Odyssey developer  Ubisoft Quebec built upon the pillars of  its predecessor and in the process shows  greater confidence in the series's new  direction taking on the role of either  Alexios or cassandra siblings and former  Spartans turned mercenaries you'll  quickly become involved in the larger  conflict of the Peloponnesian War in 431  BCE the amount of detail packed into  each location across the Greek mainland  and the islands of the Mediterranean Sea  is impressive tied together by a dynamic  ecosystem where local wildlife and  civilians keep their territory but as  you dive further you'll see the many  hardships and realities of life in  ancient Greece firsthand including the  horrors of slavery and they ever-present  war between the military driven Spartans  and the bureaucratic Athenian army I  could make use of a canary we need to  weaken their position if we are protect  to the field and drive them back to  Athens featuring a map that's more than  double the size of the previous game  Odyssey is built to be explored and as  incidental content to reward your  curiosity you get the sense that your  actions will have a lasting impact  wherever you go and Odyssey offers up a  wealth of content that fuels your growth  at a steady pace though the issue of  level gating comes up occasionally  preventing you from actively exploring  any region you wish you can take a break  from the main story and dive into the  breadth of side content and your leisure  as you traverse deeper into the world  some of Odysseys more involved missions  make themselves known in one of the more  exciting quests you'll encounter several  mythological beasts hidden within the  world offering up some of the game's  most memorable encounters where brute  force isn't always the answer  one of Odysseys more clever features is  the exploration mode with this optional  mode enabled you're challenged to use  your observation and deduction skills to  find your next target without the  support of icons or waypoints  exploration mode heightens the pride  that comes from solving puzzles and this  makes each step of your adventure feel  all the more rewarding I have my money  Markos  I'd hate to have to sell our vineyard of  course he will  Oh after there's another matter I want  to discuss with you after fine but I'm  taking a horse it's too hot too long a  walk to go on foot but think of it as  interest Marcus  everybody benefits the both Cassandra  and Alexia share the same story beats  there are differing personalities and  points of view offer unique flavor with  some scenes in questlines feeling more  appropriate with a particular character  even during their challenging  circumstances they'll still break the  tension with humor or sarcasm it's even  possible to romance side characters and  Odyssey I'm going to make you an elixir  that will give you the vigor of a man a  fraction of your AIDS while some of  these scenes can be amusing  they're often just bizarre shows of  affection that leave no lasting impact  allow me to satisfy your hunger I've  never been with a mercenary before very  well let's see what you're made of  [Music]  these awkward romance options also  appear after ordering otherwise serious  events which cheapen some of the moments  in the story but with nine possible  endings at the main story's conclusion  and the potential for characters that  die before their story is completed  there's a surprisingly large amount of  cause and effect that can make the  narrative feel all your own  in regards to its combat Odyssey no  longer features the shield's introduced  in origins and as a result action flows  at a brisker pace by placing the  emphasis more on dodging and pairing  incoming flows from enemies fighting  feels more involved in dynamic you also  have the ability to build your character  with stealth long-range and melee  specialties and respect those abilities  and anytime giving you the flexibility  to customize your play style this has  been maybe  Odyssey also brings the return of naval  combat and sailing gameplay seafaring  offers up some of the most exciting and  visually pleasing moments at the game  such as finding lost sunken ruins in the  ocean's depths we're facing off against  increasingly aggressive rival ships the  scope of Odyssey is enormous and for the  most part it's presented well but some  of the new innovations feel a bit lost  in the grand scheme of the game with  conquest battles you can pick a side in  the ongoing war between the Spartan and  Athenian armies dismantling the rivals  influence in a region while this is a  solid way of gaining resources and  improving your standing with the faction  the implementation into Odysseys general  systems make it feel mostly half-baked  the game's mechanics don't seem to take  it seriously either this is most  apparent when the main story has you  helping a particular faction despite the  site content in the area negatively  impacting them in some regard when  looking at Odyssey in the bigger picture  it can often feel like there's too much  game for its own good this is further  exacerbated by the expansive map which  can be a chore to travel through there  are also some notable bugs and hitches  that crop up throughout including those  that prevent progress and missions to  outright crashing the game several times  throughout my journey progression felt  somewhat exhausting which made some of  the more impactful and exciting moments  in the story feel like a drag despite  this the ambition within Assassin's  Creed Odyssey is admirable which is  reflected in its rich attention to  detail for the era and its approach to  handling the multi-faceted narrative  with strong protagonists at the lead  while its large-scale campaign can  occasionally be tiresome Odyssey makes  great strides in its massive and dynamic  world and the joy to venture out and  leave your mark on its ever-changing  setting  [Music]

